== Usage ==
Open [[Special:VersionWatch]] to see the latest report, or use ''Check now'' to run a new check. The page has a table for the platform (MediaWiki, PHP and the database server), one for extensions and one for skins, followed by whether the wiki is ready for the newest MediaWiki release.

=== Where extensions are looked up ===
Extensions and skins are looked up on Wikimedia Gerrit, on the release branch matching the installed MediaWiki, such as <code>REL1_43</code>; VersionWatch itself is looked up on GitHub. For an extension hosted on GitHub, maintained on another branch, or without an upstream, use the ''change'' link in the Repository column. Settings are kept as JSON on <code>MediaWiki:VersionWatch.json</code>, so changes have page history and can be reverted.

== Configuration ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Setting !! Default !! Description
|-
| <code>$wgVersionWatchGitHubToken</code> || <code>null</code> || A GitHub token, to raise GitHub's limit of 60 requests per hour per IP address. A fine-grained token without any permissions is enough.
|-
| <code>$wgVersionWatchIgnoredAuthors</code> || <code>[ 'Translation updater bot' ]</code> || Authors whose commits do not count as updates.
|}

== User rights ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Right !! Default groups !! Description
|-
| <code>versionwatch</code> || sysop || View [[Special:VersionWatch]], run a check and read the report through the API.
|}

== API ==
<code>action=query&meta=versionwatch&vwprop=summary|components|upgrade</code> returns the latest report. It does not run a check. To use it from a script, create a [[Manual:Bot passwords|bot password]] with the ''View the update status of installed software'' grant.

== Privacy ==
To check for updates, VersionWatch requests public data from gerrit.wikimedia.org, endoflife.date, api.github.com and raw.githubusercontent.com. Those requests contain the names of installed extensions and the commits they were installed from, but nothing about the wiki's users or content.

